      {
        "id": 264723,
        "name": "",
        "username": "Omegatron",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/omegatron/{size}/4904_2.png",
        "created_at": "2025-12-30T00:36:49.896Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"LonelyMan\" data-post=\"25\" data-topic=\"28888\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/l/f4b2a3/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> LonelyMan:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Christopher is wrong here, a countable infinity times another countable infinity is not an uncountable infinity. It is simply a much, much larger countable infinity.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>By definition, a countable infinity is the same “size” as the set of natural numbers. While you can have one infinity that is larger than another infinity, you can’t have a <em>countable</em> infinity that is larger than another <em>countable</em> infinity.</p>",
        "post_number": 26,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2025-12-30T00:36:49.896Z",
        "reply_count": 1,
        "reply_to_post_number": 25,
        "quote_count": 1,
