      {
        "id": 264725,
        "name": "",
        "username": "Omegatron",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/omegatron/{size}/4904_2.png",
        "created_at": "2025-12-30T11:43:57.173Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Infinities are counter intuitive.</p>\n<p>There are infinitely many natural numbers (the positive whole numbers) and there are also infinitely many integers (positive and negative whole numbers). While it may appear that there are “more” integers than natural numbers, these infinities are actually of the same “size” (cardinality).</p>\n<p>We can prove this because we can create a function to “map” each natural number to an integer on a one-to-one basis: 0 maps to 0, 1 maps to 1, 2 maps to -1, 3 maps to 2, 4 maps to -2, etc.</p>\n<p>With this mapping, it is impossible to come up with a natural number that does not correspond to an integer (or vice versa), so they must be of the same cardinality. We call any infinity of the same cardinality as the set of natural numbers a “countable infinity”, simply because the natural numbers are the ones we use to count things with.</p>",
        "post_number": 28,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2025-12-30T11:43:57.173Z",
        "reply_count": 1,
